# The law `rank/compose_decomposition.sqlc` owes: cutting the graph of which template composes
# which by directory puts every template in exactly one directory, and accounts for every edge as
# kept or crossing.
#
# Without it the measurement cannot be read
#   That relation reports the independent loops across the whole graph and the smaller number added
#   up over the directories, and concludes that the difference needs two directories to exist. It
#   would report the same thing if the cut had quietly dropped templates, and the conclusion would
#   then be about a fault rather than about the queries. This law is what makes the loss
#   structural: every template appears once whole and once under its own directory, so what the cut
#   loses is connections and never templates.
#
# Every template once, where the sibling law may repeat one
#   `algebra/decomposition.sqlc` asserts that the edges taken whole equal the edges added up over
#   the filings, because a cut by filing keeps every edge and may repeat a node. This cut is the
#   other way round: it keeps every node exactly once and drops every edge whose ends are in two
#   directories. So the node count matches exactly, and the edge count needs the crossing count to
#   balance. Reading either sum as the other kind is the mistake both headers are there to prevent.
#
# The crossing count is taken from the edges, not by subtraction
#   The whole less the kept would be true by construction and would check nothing. The third term
#   is counted directly off `rank/compose_edges.sqlc`, by comparing the two ends' directories, so
#   the identity has three terms worked out separately and can fail.
#
# The directory rule is shared with `rank/compose_measures.sqlc`, and the counting is not
#   What a directory is has to be one definition, or the two sides measure different cuts, so the
#   `CASE` naming a template at the root `'(root)'` is written the same way in both. What is
#   independent is the route to the number: the relation reaches it through a scoped join and a walk
#   that ignores direction, and this reaches it by comparing two ends pairwise.
#
# Probe
#   Drop the templates at the root from the scope list in `rank/compose_measures.sqlc` and
#   recompose: the node count must fail, and the loop count, which this law does not mention, must
#   not move.
-- algebra/roster.sqlc against rank/compose_measures.sqlc: the directory cut puts each template in
-- one directory.
SELECT a.slug AS law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT 'rank/compose_decomposition' AS subject,
           x.whole_nodes = x.cut_nodes
             AND x.whole_edges = x.cut_edges + x.crossing                            AS holds,
           format('%s templates whole, %s over %s directories; %s edges, %s kept, %s crossing',
                  x.whole_nodes, x.cut_nodes, x.directories,
                  x.whole_edges, x.cut_edges, x.crossing)                            AS detail
    FROM ( SELECT
             (SELECT m.n_nodes FROM ( :compose(rank/compose_measures.sqlc) ) m
               WHERE m.scope IS NULL)                                                AS whole_nodes,
             (SELECT coalesce(sum(m.n_nodes), 0) FROM ( :compose(rank/compose_measures.sqlc) ) m
               WHERE m.scope IS NOT NULL)                                            AS cut_nodes,
             (SELECT m.m_edges FROM ( :compose(rank/compose_measures.sqlc) ) m
               WHERE m.scope IS NULL)                                                AS whole_edges,
             (SELECT coalesce(sum(m.m_edges), 0) FROM ( :compose(rank/compose_measures.sqlc) ) m
               WHERE m.scope IS NOT NULL)                                            AS cut_edges,
             (SELECT count(*) FROM ( :compose(rank/compose_edges.sqlc) ) e
               WHERE (CASE WHEN position('/' IN e.parent) > 0
                           THEN split_part(e.parent, '/', 1) ELSE '(root)' END)
                  <> (CASE WHEN position('/' IN e.child) > 0
                           THEN split_part(e.child, '/', 1) ELSE '(root)' END))       AS crossing,
             (SELECT count(*) FROM ( :compose(rank/compose_measures.sqlc) ) m
               WHERE m.scope IS NOT NULL)                                            AS directories
         ) x
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'compose_decomposition'
